  "question": "What Greek term does Calvin translate as “mortificationem,” and what reason does he give for preferring this rendering over “dying”?",
  "answer": "Calvin translates the Greek word nekrosin as “mortificationem.” He argues that this rendering more fully conveys the apostle’s meaning because it emphasizes the idea of “putting to death” rather than merely “dying.” In Calvin’s view, the term highlights the humiliating and painful sufferings of the apostles, which serve as an outward conformity to Christ’s own violent death. As the passage notes, “The term mortification, when taken in strict accordance with its etymology, in the sense of putting to death, appears to bring out more fully the apostle's meaning, than the word ‘dying,’ made use of in our authorized version.” [Calvin — Commentary 2Corinthians]
